A population of 500 bacteria will triple in 20 hours.
Using the formula given below,
\[
A=P e^{r t}
\]
\(A=\) population after \(t\) hours
\(P=\) initial population
\(r=\) rate of growth
\(t=\) time in hours
\begin{itemize}
\item[a)] Determine the rate of growth, \(r\).
\item[b)] Determine how many hours it will take for the initial population to double with the same rate of growth.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Solution}
\begin{itemize}
\item[a)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
1500 & =500 e^{r(20)} \\
3 & =e^{20 r} \\
\ln 3 & =\ln e^{20 r} \\
\ln 3 & =20 r \cdot \ln e \\
r & =\frac{\ln 3}{20} \\
r & =0.054930614
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for substitution
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for applying logarithms
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for power rule
\(\frac{1}{2}\) mark for evaluating quotient of logarithms
2 marks
\begin{itemize}
\item[b)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
1000 & =500 e^{0.054930614 t} \\
2 & =e^{0.054930614 t} \\
\ln 2 & =\ln e^{0.054930614 t} \\
\ln 2 & =0.054930614 t \cdot \ln e \\
t & =\frac{\ln 2}{0.054930614} \\
t & =12.619 \text { hours }
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
