Solve the following equation:
\[
\log _{4}(x+2)+\log _{4} 3=\log _{4} x
\]

\section*{Solution}

Method 1
\[
\begin{aligned}
\log _{4}(x+2)+\log _{4} 3 & =\log _{4} x \\
\log _{4}[(x+2) 3] & =\log _{4} x \\
3(x+2) & =x \\
3 x+6 & =x \\
x & =-3
\end{aligned}
\]
No solution

Method 2
\[
\begin{aligned}
\log _{4}(x+2)+\log _{4} 3 & =\log _{4} x \\
\log _{4}(x+2)+\log _{4} 3-\log _{4} x & =0 \\
\log _{4}\left[\frac{3(x+2)}{x}\right] & =0 \\
4^{0} & =\frac{3 x+6}{x} \\
x & =-3 \\
x & >-3
\end{aligned}
\]

