\begin{itemize}
\item[a)] Given the functions \(f(x)=\sqrt{4+x}\) and \(g(x)=|3 x-6|\), evaluate \(f(g(-5))\).
\item[b)] Is it possible to evaluate \(g(f(-5))\) ?
Justify your answer.
\end{itemize}

Solution
\begin{itemize}
\item[a)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
g(-5) & =|3(-5)-6| \\
g(-5) & =21 \\
f(21) & =\sqrt{4+21} \\
& =\sqrt{25} \\
& =5
\end{aligned}
\]
1 mark for consistent value of \(f(g(-5))\)
2 marks
\item[b)] No, because \(f(x)\) is undefined when \(x=-5\) 1 mark for justification
or
\[
\begin{aligned}
& f(-5)=\sqrt{4+(-5)} \\
& f(-5)=\sqrt{-1}
\end{aligned}
\]
1 mark
\end{itemize}
\(f(-5)\) is undefined because you cannot evaluate the square root of a negative number.

